I'm going to hit that L key

262
00:10:37.720 --> 00:10:40.590
to get that lerp back in again.

263
00:10:40.590 --> 00:10:43.230
And I'm going to pull in the RGB for the wood

264
00:10:43.230 --> 00:10:44.950
and put it on the A channel

265
00:10:44.950 --> 00:10:46.230
and make sure you're consistent

266
00:10:46.230 --> 00:10:48.350
as you know where things go

267
00:10:48.350 --> 00:10:51.710
and how you want things to look.

268
00:10:51.710 --> 00:10:53.190
And then I'm going to put my gold.

269
00:10:53.190 --> 00:10:54.110
And what I mean by that is

270
00:10:54.110 --> 00:10:55.870
if you're going to put gold in the B channel of lerp,

271
00:10:55.870 --> 00:10:59.370
make sure it's there for everyone.

272
00:10:59.370 --> 00:11:01.129
And then I'm going to grab this here,

273
00:11:01.129 --> 00:11:02.970
one minus and put it in the alpha.

274
00:11:02.970 --> 00:11:04.210
And again, we can see a preview.

275
00:11:04.210 --> 00:11:05.549
What does that look like?

276
00:11:05.549 --> 00:11:06.389
There you go.

277
00:11:06.389 --> 00:11:07.450
That's our intention.

278
00:11:07.450 --> 00:11:14.820
And now we're going to pull this all the way to our rough.

279
00:11:14.860 --> 00:11:16.240
Ooh, look at that.

280
00:11:16.240 --> 00:11:17.419
That looks great.

281
00:11:17.419 --> 00:11:19.399
It's going in the right direction.

282
00:11:19.399 --> 00:11:21.740
Kind of like our intentions.

283
00:11:21.740 --> 00:11:23.460
Now at this point, if you wanted to,

284
00:11:23.460 --> 00:11:25.179
you could pull in specular and say,

285
00:11:25.179 --> 00:11:27.740
you know what, I'm going to make this 0.5

286
00:11:27.740 --> 00:11:29.259
like the default.

287
00:11:29.259 --> 00:11:31.340
And hey, you get a shiny floor.

288
00:11:31.340 --> 00:11:32.679
And believe it or not,

289
00:11:32.679 --> 00:11:35.500
even though we have specular in there,

290
00:11:35.500 --> 00:11:37.600
our rough is still being honored.

291
00:11:37.600 --> 00:11:38.740
So you do need to be careful though.

292
00:11:38.740 --> 00:11:42.340
Sometimes spec can mute rough and vice versa,

293
00:11:42.340 --> 00:11:43.960
depending on your values

294
00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:45.960
and what your intentions are,

295
00:11:45.960 --> 00:11:47.200
they may not come out the way you want,

296
00:11:47.200 --> 00:11:49.159
but this actually comes up pretty good.

297
00:11:49.159 --> 00:11:54.000
Putting just a half specular in there, a 0.5.

298
00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:58.600
Now let's go ahead and round things out here.

299
00:11:58.600 --> 00:11:59.639
And we're going to go in here

300
00:11:59.639 --> 00:12:00.860
and do a little bit of normal.

301
00:12:00.860 --> 00:12:04.639
I'm going to introduce you to a new type of node.

302
00:12:04.639 --> 00:12:06.139
And I don't think we talked about this

303
00:12:06.139 --> 00:12:08.549
in the first class.

304
00:12:08.549 --> 00:12:12.159
So I'm going to go in here and grab those normals,

305
00:12:12.159 --> 00:12:14.610
pull them over.

306
00:12:14.610 --> 00:12:19.220
And now what we can do is two things.

307
00:12:19.220 --> 00:12:22.779
One of them, I can do the same process where I have a lerp

308
00:12:22.779 --> 00:12:27.759
and in here, I can grab that texture that we had before.

309
00:12:27.759 --> 00:12:30.620
Control D.

310
00:12:30.620 --> 00:12:33.019
And I can, if I want to,

311
00:12:33.019 --> 00:12:35.659
I can one minus it and plug these all within the lerp

312
00:12:35.659 --> 00:12:37.519
like we did before.

313
00:12:37.519 --> 00:12:40.460
Another way we can do it actually works pretty well.

314
00:12:40.460 --> 00:12:44.090
Let me move these out of the way

315
00:12:44.090 --> 00:12:48.120
because it's going to honor two different normals

316
00:12:48.120 --> 00:12:50.879
and they're in the right spot texture wise.

317
00:12:50.879 --> 00:12:55.629
I can go in here and create a blend.

318
00:12:55.629 --> 00:12:57.070
This is a material function again,

319
00:12:57.070 --> 00:13:01.940
blend angle corrected normals.

320
00:13:01.940 --> 00:13:04.419
And this actually is a bit more efficient.

321
00:13:04.419 --> 00:13:05.500
And what I mean by that,

322
00:13:05.500 --> 00:13:08.019
it honors one normal change into another

323
00:13:08.019 --> 00:13:11.179
and it blends them pretty well so that the edges

324
00:13:11.179 --> 00:13:14.299
and the differences in how the angles are created

325
00:13:14.299 --> 00:13:17.100
and corrected according to where the camera's positioned,

326
00:13:17.100 --> 00:13:19.940
it actually makes it so it has a nice transition.

327
00:13:19.940 --> 00:13:24.269
So it doesn't look too harsh, obscure or sloppy.

328
00:13:24.269 --> 00:13:26.850
So our base is going to be our wood.

329
00:13:26.850 --> 00:13:29.370
We want our wood to be our background

330
00:13:29.370 --> 00:13:33.090
and then our additional normal is going to be our gold.

331
00:13:33.090 --> 00:13:35.429
And then now we're going to add that.

332
00:13:35.429 --> 00:13:37.309
And it's not like a huge difference here

333
00:13:37.309 --> 00:13:39.830
just because the gold doesn't really have

334
00:13:39.830 --> 00:13:41.250
a lot of normal value.

335
00:13:41.250 --> 00:13:42.590
But if you're working with other things,

336
00:13:42.590 --> 00:13:44.070
this can be very helpful,

337
00:13:44.070 --> 00:13:46.230
especially with one stronger normals.

338
00:13:46.230 --> 00:13:49.029
I'm going to put that in the normal there.

339
00:13:49.029 --> 00:13:50.269
Cool, if anything,

340
00:13:50.269 --> 00:13:52.470
it highlights a little bit of the edge more

341
00:13:52.470 --> 00:13:56.470
between the differences between the gold on the floor

342
00:13:56.470 --> 00:13:59.129
and actually works pretty well.

343
00:13:59.129 --> 00:14:02.370
And we'll hit apply.

344
00:14:02.370 --> 00:14:03.210
There you go.

345
00:14:03.210 --> 00:14:07.409
So we've just masked out using a simple system here

346
00:14:07.409 --> 00:14:10.990
of lerps and we're not even using a masking component,

347
00:14:10.990 --> 00:14:14.129
which is something different we get into another classes.

348
00:14:14.129 --> 00:14:14.970
But in here,

349
00:14:14.970 --> 00:14:17.529
we got these all masked out and have two different

350
00:14:17.529 --> 00:14:19.649
textures that make it look like two different materials

351
00:14:19.649 --> 00:14:23.899
on top of each other, pretty neat.

352
00:14:23.899 --> 00:14:27.700
Another way to be able to do this is using your RGBs,

353
00:14:27.700 --> 00:14:29.259
which I mentioned earlier.

354
00:14:29.259 --> 00:14:31.860
And you can embed this right within a texture.

355
00:14:31.860 --> 00:14:33.259
Let me show you what I mean.

356
00:14:33.259 --> 00:14:34.740
I have a texture set up in Unreal

357
00:14:34.740 --> 00:14:36.549
that we can take a peek at.

358
00:14:36.549 --> 00:14:39.190
In Unreal, you'll see three different types

359
00:14:39.190 --> 00:14:43.149
of texture values and looks here.

360
00:14:43.149 --> 00:14:46.399
And all of these are actually within one texture.

361
00:14:46.399 --> 00:14:49.720
I have a rough here and I have another one here,

362
00:14:49.720 --> 00:14:51.980
which is working in another color channel

363
00:14:51.980 --> 00:14:52.840
and another one here,

364
00:14:52.840 --> 00:14:56.879
which is working in another of those RGB color channels.

365
00:14:56.879 --> 00:14:59.100
And let's actually look at that up close.

366
00:14:59.100 --> 00:15:00.679
So if I double click on the material,

367
00:15:00.679 --> 00:15:04.909
you'll see that I'm using my red channel

368
00:15:04.909 --> 00:15:09.629
for my roughness and my B channel for my base color.

369
00:15:09.629 --> 00:15:10.470
If I wanted to,

370
00:15:10.470 --> 00:15:14.070
I could even do base color for my,

371
00:15:14.070 --> 00:15:16.289
using the color that I have placed in here.

372
00:15:17.009 --> 00:15:18.490
In this layer in Photoshop,

373
00:15:18.490 --> 00:15:20.649
I can color that any way that I want.

374
00:15:20.649 --> 00:15:22.889
I did it super generic,

375
00:15:22.889 --> 00:15:25.460
but you'll see if we do flat,

376
00:15:25.460 --> 00:15:28.299
you'll see I've just have some just simple green

377
00:15:28.299 --> 00:15:31.980
and blue and those can be anything that you need and want

378
00:15:31.980 --> 00:15:34.940
and you can even mass them out like you saw me do

379
00:15:34.940 --> 00:15:38.059
on the last little demo.

380
00:15:38.059 --> 00:15:39.779
So all of this is up to you.

381
00:15:39.779 --> 00:15:41.659
It just depends on what you have.

382
00:15:41.659 --> 00:15:45.500
But the nice thing about this is we also can go in here

383
00:15:45.500 --> 00:15:46.539
and say, hey, you know what?

384
00:15:46.700 --> 00:15:50.899
I wanna do the metallic to be green, you know,

385
00:15:50.899 --> 00:15:55.860
and I want, hey, no, maybe I want that to be blue instead.

386
00:15:55.860 --> 00:15:58.980
You can actually tweak these out and play with them a bit,

387
00:15:58.980 --> 00:16:00.580
but let's look at the texture up close

388
00:16:00.580 --> 00:16:02.500
and I'll show you what I'm talking about.

389
00:16:02.500 --> 00:16:06.620
So this, I'm gonna turn all the RGB stuff off.

390
00:16:06.620 --> 00:16:09.039
I'm gonna zoom out a little bit.

391
00:16:09.039 --> 00:16:11.279
And if I click on my alpha,

392
00:16:11.279 --> 00:16:16.500
you'll see I have an alpha texture embedded in here

393
00:16:16.500 --> 00:16:19.940
and I could use that within a one texture

394
00:16:19.940 --> 00:16:22.179
or in one material.

395
00:16:22.179 --> 00:16:25.139
I have a blue channel here.

396
00:16:25.139 --> 00:16:27.940
So in this blue channel, I have a black and white values

397
00:16:27.940 --> 00:16:31.590
we could use for height or we can use for bump.

398
00:16:31.590 --> 00:16:33.750
We have a green one in here,

399
00:16:33.750 --> 00:16:38.450
the same or some other intention

400
00:16:38.450 --> 00:16:41.860
and the R channel, I have rough.

401
00:16:41.860 --> 00:16:43.820
This can be our roughness or dirt

402
00:16:43.820 --> 00:16:45.600
or whatever it is that you wanna use it for,

403
00:16:45.600 --> 00:16:47.019
it's available.

404
00:16:47.580 --> 00:16:49.059
So it's actually really cool to be able to have this

405
00:16:49.059 --> 00:16:53.919
because then you can plug and play whatever you want.

406
00:16:53.919 --> 00:16:55.519
So I'm gonna disconnect this one

407
00:16:55.519 --> 00:17:00.070
and you can simply just Alt click, left click

408
00:17:00.070 --> 00:17:02.750
to get rid of that if you're not aware of it.

409
00:17:02.750 --> 00:17:05.349
And then we can change these any way that we need.

410
00:17:05.349 --> 00:17:06.470
There's like even emissive,

411
00:17:06.470 --> 00:17:08.250
maybe you want emissive in there.

412
00:17:08.250 --> 00:17:10.089
It's all within this texture

413
00:17:10.089 --> 00:17:13.150
and then you don't have to load several different 4K,

414
00:17:13.150 --> 00:17:15.230
8K textures, you can actually work off

415
00:17:15.230 --> 00:17:17.470
of one particular texture that's packed accordingly.

416
00:17:17.990 --> 00:17:19.190
But keep in mind when you do this,

417
00:17:19.190 --> 00:17:21.750
you have to plan ahead of time

418
00:17:21.750 --> 00:17:23.990
because you have to be very deterministic

419
00:17:23.990 --> 00:17:26.109
and very strategic when doing this.

420
00:17:26.109 --> 00:17:28.460
You need to know exactly what you're going for.

421
00:17:28.460 --> 00:17:31.119
Here's a link to some other resources

422
00:17:31.119 --> 00:17:32.400
you can take a look at,

423
00:17:32.400 --> 00:17:33.819
which will point you in the right direction,

424
00:17:33.819 --> 00:17:36.339
looking at how to mask your textures

425
00:17:36.339 --> 00:17:40.299
as well as one of our former employees, Tor Frick,

426
00:17:40.299 --> 00:17:43.240
who has an amazing demo from way back

427
00:17:43.240 --> 00:17:46.660
in which he's only using like three textures.

428
00:17:46.660 --> 00:17:47.779
It's actually pretty cool.

429
00:17:47.779 --> 00:17:51.019
And he's masked them accordingly using those RGB channels.

430
00:17:51.019 --> 00:17:52.940
Well, that's it for this lesson.

431
00:17:52.940 --> 00:17:53.859
In the next lesson,

432
00:17:53.859 --> 00:17:57.019
we're gonna take a look at material functions

433
00:17:57.019 --> 00:17:59.819
and I'm gonna show you how to build one for yourself.
